 * is identical for every invocation.
 *
 * Watchdog wiring
 * ---------------
 *   - Every NDJSON line we successfully parse from `stdout` resets the
 *     idle timer. A process that is "thinking" (sending `message_*`
 *     events) never trips the watchdog.
 *   - Every `stderr` chunk resets the timer too. Bash subprocesses
 *     often print progress to stderr.
 *   - On `close` or `error`, the timer is cleared (process is gone).
 *   - When the timer fires we:
 *       1. Freeze the whole process group via SIGSTOP (POSIX) or, on
 *          win32 where SIGSTOP doesn't exist, immediately SIGKILL it
 *          and return as a failed exit (no suspension registered — see
 *          the win32 note below).
 *       2. Snapshot the partial transcript (the `messages` and `stderr`
 *          accumulated so far) and bundle it with the resume-context
 *          (`Job`) the parent caller passed in.
 *       3. Detach the abort handler so a user Ctrl+C won't try to
 *          SIGTERM a stopped process (SIGTERM is queued, not delivered,
 *          until SIGCONT — which would race with our resume/kill).
 *       4. Register the Suspension, then resolve with kind="suspended".
 *
 * Win32 fallback (no SIGSTOP)
 * ---------------------------
 * Windows has no job-freeze primitive in the POSIX sense. We could
 * `SuspendThread` but that only freezes one thread inside the sub-`pi`
 * process — its children (bash, deadlock-grandchildren) keep running,
 * which defeats the point. So on win32 the watchdog simply
 * `killProcessGroup`s the child, marks the run as a failed exit
 * (exitCode=-1, stopReason="aborted"), and returns. No suspension is
 * registered and no arbitration is offered. This is documented in the
 * watchdog comment so future readers know it is a deliberate decision,
 * not an oversight.
